import re

MASSES = {"H": 1.008, "C": 12.011, "N": 14.007, "O": 15.999, "Na": 22.990, "Cl": 35.45}

def molar_mass(formula):
    return sum(MASSES[el] * (int(n) if n else 1) for el, n in re.findall(r"([A-Z][a-z]?)(\d*)", formula))

def atom_economy(reactants, product):
    return molar_mass(product) / sum(molar_mass(f) for f in reactants)

exercises.solution:
```python
import re

MASSES = {"H": 1.008, "C": 12.011, "N": 14.007, "O": 15.999, "Na": 22.990, "Cl": 35.45}

def molar_mass(formula):
    return sum(MASSES[el] * (int(n) if n else 1)
               for el, n in re.findall(r"([A-Z][a-z]?)(\d*)", formula))

def atom_economy(reactants, product):
    return molar_mass(product) / sum(molar_mass(f) for f in reactants)
```

The pattern `[A-Z][a-z]?` reads one capital letter and an optional lower-case one, so `Na` and `Cl` are single symbols. Paracetamol from 4-aminophenol and ethanoic anhydride has an atom economy of 71.6%: the by-product is one molecule of ethanoic acid. An esterification loses only water (89.3% for ethyl benzoate), and an addition such as the Diels–Alder reaction of buta-1,3-diene with ethene keeps every atom (100%). Atom economy assumes perfect yields and ignores solvents, so it is one measure among several; process mass intensity counts everything.
